## The landing page carries no stat row

**Found 2026-09-04 (design steer #2, pass 9).** `HomeView` receives no billing data, so the four
cards that filled its `StatsGrid` could only describe the app that was rendering them. Deleted
rather than rewritten (`57085f8`): a card that counts the app is a card the customer cannot read,
and the lede plus the route cards already do the page's job. `appName` went with it, so
`routes/index.tsx` no longer passes it. If the landing page ever gets a real number — open runs,
collected this period — it should come back as a `WidgetCard` with a hero, on data this layer
actually has.

## A page hero needs a step the card hero is not already using

**Found 2026-09-04 (design steer #2, pass 11).** The critique's hierarchy finding (§5) was that
every number on a product screen carries the same weight. Its six ranked items were closed by
earlier passes, but this one had nothing to spend: `--ns-text-4xl` is the card hero, so a page that
needed a larger figure had nowhere to step, and the console read as a grid of equal tiles.

Added `--ns-text-display` (3rem) and a card modifier that spends it — on the card, not on the Hero
band, which is what `widgets-card.tsx` said the fix would have to be: the size a hero renders at is
decided by where the card sits on the page, and the card is what knows that. One card per page takes
it (`billed-summary` on `/runs`, `run-summary` on `/runs/[id]`, each the first card on its page).

## The critique named the wrong token, and the right one was invisible

**Found 2026-09-04 (design steer #2, pass 11).** Critique §2.1 said `--ns-paper-6` and
`--ns-paper-7` "lack sufficient contrast when used for labels and axis ticks". Measured, they do
not: labels and axis ticks use `--ns-muted-fg`, which is paper-7 at **6.54:1** on paper-1 and
**6.82:1** on a card in light, and **4.81:1** on a dark card. Both clear AA.

The token that actually fails is the one the critique did not name. `--ns-absent` — the em dash, the
product's "not measured" — was paper-5 at **2.35:1** on a light card and **2.16:1** on a dark one,
under the 3:1 that even a large glyph needs. Every absence in the product was a fact the reader
could not see, which makes it the same defect as printing `0.00`: the value is there and it does not
reach anyone.

Stepped to paper-6 in light (**4.03:1**) and paper-8 in dark (**4.81:1**, which clears the
small-text bar). Light cannot clear 4.5:1 and that is a ramp constraint, not a choice:
`--ns-money-pos` is **4.90:1** here, so any tone dark enough to reach 4.5:1 would out-shout the real
amount the dash is standing in for. Dark has the headroom because its money ramp steps to
**7.00:1**. Closing the gap in light means darkening the money ramp first, which is a theme decision
for the owner.

The numbers are now a test (`M1 — the absent tone is visible and stays quieter than a real amount`),
which converts OKLCH to sRGB and computes WCAG luminance for both themes. It fails at the old value
(2.35:1) and asserts both the floor and the ceiling, so the constraint above survives whoever edits
the ramp next.

## Correction: `ratio()` and `formatPercent()` are not dead

**Found 2026-09-04 (design steer #2, pass 11), correcting pass 10.** Pass 10 recorded that
`lib/money.ts` exports `ratio()` and `formatPercent()` with "zero production call sites" and
recommended deleting both. They have one: `movement()` at `lib/money.ts:345` calls both, and
`movement()` backs the DeltaChip on the runs console headline and in `run-comparison`. The earlier
sweep excluded `lib/money.ts` from its own grep, which is what hid the only call site there is.
Deleting them would have taken the delta chip down with them. Nothing was deleted; the finding is
withdrawn.
